### Summary of Key Insights
The retrieved evidence demonstrates that medical guidelines, such as those from the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (ACOG), actually recommend delayed umbilical cord clamping for most newborns to maximize the transfer of beneficial blood, stem cells, and antibodies. Immediate cord clamping is not a routine practice designed to deny immunity, but rather a clinical intervention reserved strictly for medical emergencies, such as placental abruption or maternal hemorrhage. Furthermore, the passive immunity a baby receives from the mother is inherently temporary and does not provide permanent protection against vaccine-preventable diseases. Because this natural protection naturally fades within weeks or months, childhood vaccinations are required to stimulate the baby's own active, long-term immune response, completely independent of the timing of cord clamping.

### Conclusion
Based on the recorded evidence, the claim is `refuted`. Medical professionals do not routinely cut the umbilical cord immediately to deny babies natural immunity; in fact, standard guidelines advocate for delayed clamping to ensure the baby receives these natural benefits. Immediate clamping is only utilized in life-threatening emergencies. Additionally, because maternal passive immunity is naturally short-lived and incomplete, vaccines are medically necessary to establish long-term active immunity, exposing the claim's conspiracy narrative as entirely false.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The claim is refuted because standard medical guidelines from the [American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (ACOG)](https://www.acog.org/womens-health/experts-and-stories/ask-acog/what-is-delayed-cord-clamping) actually recommend delayed umbilical cord clamping for at least 30 to 60 seconds for most babies to maximize the transfer of beneficial blood, stem cells, and antibodies. A [University of Rhode Island study](https://www.uri.edu/news/2019/02/uri-nursing-study-shows-for-first-time-benefits-of-delayed-umbilical-cord-clamping-on-brain-development-in-healthy-babies/) highlights that this delay provides significant physiological benefits, such as increased iron stores and brain myelin development. Immediate cord clamping is not a routine practice to deny immunity, but a clinical intervention reserved strictly for medical emergencies like placental abruption or maternal hemorrhage, as outlined by [CPQCC guidelines](https://www.cpqcc.org/sites/default/files/Delayed%20Cord%20Clamping%20Guidelines%202.3.16.pdf). Furthermore, research from [Weill Cornell Medicine](https://news.weill.cornell.edu/news/2019/06/breaking-the-code-how-is-a-mother%E2%80%99s-immunity-transferred-to-her-baby) and the [NHS](https://www.nhs.uk/common-health-questions/childrens-health/how-long-do-babies-carry-their-mothers-immunity/) shows that maternal passive immunity is temporary and does not offer permanent protection against vaccine-preventable diseases; therefore, childhood vaccinations are medically necessary to stimulate active, long-term immunity as maternal antibodies naturally fade, regardless of when the cord is clamped.